--TEST--
`Foo::class` is the fully qualified name of the class, folded to a `string` constant at compile time — `rule:statements/storage-that-outlives-a-call`'s "inlined at every use site, with no storage to read it back from", resolved the way every other class side is
--FILE--
<?nvs
namespace App;

// The written name is resolved against the file's namespace and imports,
// exactly as a static call's class side is, so the answer is the fully
// qualified name and never the short one PHP would also refuse to give.
class User {
    // `self` is the class this method is declared in, and stays folded for
    // that reason. `static::class` is the other spelling and answers the
    // *called* class instead — `rule:types/class-constant`, pinned by its own case.
    public static function own(): string {
        return self::class;
    }
}

class Admin extends User {
    // `parent` is the first `extends` link — the same resolution
    // `parent::method()` already takes.
    public static function inherited(): string {
        return parent::class;
    }
}

echo User::class, "\n";
echo Admin::class, "\n";
echo Admin::own(), "\n";
echo Admin::inherited(), "\n";

// It is a `string`, not a class handle: it binds, concatenates and
// compares like any other one. `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants` has no `new $name` for it to
// reach, which is why this is all it is.
string $s = User::class;
echo $s . "!", "\n";
echo (User::class == "App\\User") as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
App\User
App\Admin
App\User
App\User
App\User!
1
